<h1><span lang="EN-GB">New Plane - Non-Interactive</span></h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span lang="EN-US">This command is accessible from</span> <span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b><i style="font-style:normal;">Datuming &gt; Non-Interactive &gt; Plane</i></b>.</span></p>
<p class="description"><i style="font-style:italic; font-weight:bold;">Description:</i> New Plane command creates new datum plane.</p>
<p class="description" style="font-style:normal;"><i style="font-style:normal;"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Plane_-_main_screenshot.GIF" width="449px" height="360px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></span></i></p>
<p class="parameterstitle">Common parts of the dialog:</p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Name</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– name for the plane. The default name is ‘Plane N’, where N is the number of the subsequent newly created datum plane.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><b style="font-weight:bold;"><span lang="EN-GB">Result</span>: When all parameters are entered or all necessary entities are selected from the lists, the result, which contains the co-ordinates of the plane normal and the offset from plane centre to the origin, is displayed:</b></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image026.jpg" width="451px" height="106px" border="0" class="img_whs5" alt=""></span></p>
<h2 class="normal" style="font-style:normal;"><span style="font-style:normal;"><i><span lang="EN-US" style="font-style:normal;">M</span></i><i style="font-style:normal;"><span lang="EN-US">ethods:</span></i></span></h2>
<p class="sub-head">Point and Normal</p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Plane_-_Point_and_Normal.GIF" width="409px" height="141px" border="0" class="img_whs6" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select and existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list or type-in co-ordinates of the centre point of the plane.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Normal</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select and existing datum line or cylinder from the list or type-in the co-ordinates of the plane normal.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">P</span>oint defines the plane centre and Normal – its orientation. In case of sphere or circle its centre is used as point. In case of cylinder, its axis is used as line.</p>
<p class="Note" id="p1"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Note</b></span>: Despite this method uses the same entities as ‘Line And Point’ method, the plane is created in different way.</p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Three Points</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image018.jpg" width="408px" height="119px" border="0" class="img_whs7" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 3</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">Point 1, Point 2, and Point 3 must be three different points and should not have to be collinear.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">In case of sphere and circle, their centres are used as points.</span></p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Line and Point</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image020.jpg" width="408px" height="122px" border="0" class="img_whs8" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Line</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum line or cylinder from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">T</span>he point and both ends of the line will lie on the created plane. The point should not have to be contained within the line. In case of sphere or circle its centre is used as point. In case of cylinder, its axis is used as line.</p>
<p class="Note" id="p2"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Note</b></span>: Despite this method uses the same entities as ‘Point And Normal’ method, the plane is created in different way.</p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Two Lines</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image022.jpg" width="406px" height="117px" border="0" class="img_whs9" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Line 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum line or cylinder from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Line 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum line or cylinder from the list.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">Line 1and Line 2 must be two different lines that are co-planar, but are non-collinear.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">In case of cylinders, their axes are used as lines.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Circle</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image024.jpg" width="408px" height="74px" border="0" class="img_whs10" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Circle</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB">The plane is defined as the circle’s plane. Plane centre will coincide with the circle centre.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-US">Points and Offsets</span></p>
<p class="whs11"><span style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;"><span lang="EN-US"><span lang="EN-US"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Plane_-_Points_and_Offsets.GIF" width="410px" height="121px" border="0" class="img_whs12" alt=""></span></span></font></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Offset 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– enter the positive or negative offset in mm for</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span><span lang="EN-US">.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Offset 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– enter the positive or negative offset in mm for</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span><span lang="EN-US">.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 3</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Offset 3</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– enter the positive or negative offset in mm for</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 3</b></span><span lang="EN-US">.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">Every point will be at a distance to the newly created plane, equal to the corresponding offset. There are two possible solutions – the plane can be constructed above or below the three points, so the order of the selected points determines where the plane to be created and follows the right-hand rule. To get the second solution, simply select the points in the reverse order.</font></span></p>
<p><img src="using_datums_files/plane_-_points_and_offsets_-_scheme.gif" width="490px" height="408px" border="0" class="img_whs13" alt=""></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">There are some relations that must be observed in order to construct plane using this method:</font></span></p>
<p><i style="font-style:italic;"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">| Offset 3 – Offset 1 |&#160; &lt;&#160; distance ( Point 3, Point 1)</font></span></i></p>
<p><i style="font-style:italic;"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">| Offset 2 – Offset 1 |&#160; &lt;&#160; distance ( Point 2, Point 1)</font></span></i></p>
<p><i style="font-style:italic;"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">| Offset 3 – Offset 2 |&#160; &lt;&#160; distance ( Point 3, Point 2)</font></span></i></p>
<p>&#160;</p>
